The dashed vertical lines at \(x=0\) and \(x=1\) mark the response range; naive linear (dashed gray) is the only density that isn’t actually confined inside them – exactly the failure mode its widget above shows for the mean curve, now visible in the distribution itself. The other 3 densities all integrate to 1 strictly within \([0,1]\): Beta IRT’s shifts left/right with \(\delta_j\) without changing shape, but narrows or widens in place as \(\tau_j\) moves – large \(\tau_j\) concentrates it tightly around the mean, small (or negative) \(\tau_j\) spreads it out and can even pull mass toward both boundaries at once, all while the mean itself never moves, exactly as the equation in Model equations implies. Samejima CRM’s and Mueller CRSM’s shapes visibly skew as \(b\) moves them toward either boundary, a consequence of the nonlinear backtransform in both models that a mean-curve-only view can’t show. Moving \(a\) redistributes naive linear’s and Samejima CRM’s mass without translating it (steeper slope on the same \(\theta\)), and does nothing to Beta IRT’s or Mueller CRSM’s density, consistent with neither model having a discrimination parameter.


Comparing information curves across models
The densities above show where probability mass sits; item information shows how precisely each model can pin down \(\theta\) from a response, as a function of \(\theta\) itself. This is worth looking at on its own, because information curves can diverge sharply across these 4 models even for items with matched difficulty/discrimination, in a way the mean and density widgets above don’t make obvious – a point raised directly by Dylan Molenaar (author of the BoundIRT package discussed in Limitations (Molenaar 2026)) in feedback on an earlier draft of this vignette.
Fisher information is computed the same way for all 4 models here, without relying on a model-specific closed form: \(I(\theta) = E\!\left[\left(\dfrac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log
f(X\mid\theta)\right)^{2}\right]\), with the score obtained by central finite difference on each model’s own log-density (the same *Density() functions used for the plots above) and the expectation evaluated by numerical integration over \(X\). This is a numerical approximation, not an analytically derived information function – adequate for a qualitative comparison of shape across models, but not a substitute for each model’s published closed-form information function if a precise value is needed.


Code
function scoreSquaredTimesDensity(x, theta, densityOfTheta, h) {
  const fC = densityOfTheta(x, theta);
  if (fC &lt;= 1e-10) return 0;
  const fP = densityOfTheta(x, theta + h);
  const fM = densityOfTheta(x, theta - h);
  const logFP = Math.log(Math.max(fP, 1e-300));
  const logFM = Math.log(Math.max(fM, 1e-300));
  const score = (logFP - logFM) / (2 * h);
  return score * score * fC;
}

function fisherInformationAt(densityOfTheta, theta, lo, hi, n, h = 0.001) {
  return simpson((x) =&gt; scoreSquaredTimesDensity(x, theta, densityOfTheta, h), lo, hi, n);
}

// For the 3 bounded-response models, integrating over x directly on a fixed
// grid (as fisherInformationAt above does) silently fails at extreme
// parameter values: a steep model-implied slope can concentrate almost all
// response density into an x-window far narrower than the grid resolves,
// so the integral is undercounted and the curve falsely collapses toward 0
// -- caught by checking Samejima CRM against its own closed form (Fisher
// info there is analytically alpha^2/sigma^2, exactly constant in theta,
// since Z is a Gaussian location family and Fisher information is
// invariant under the 1-to-1 Z-&gt;X transform); the naive grid showed a false
// collapse near the theta range's edges at high slider values where the
// true curve is flat. Substituting z = logit(x) as the integration
// variable fixes this generically: a spike near x=0 or x=1 becomes a
// normal-width bump on the z scale, which a uniform grid over z resolves
// correctly regardless of how extreme the original x-concentration was.
function fisherInformationBoundedAt(densityOfTheta, theta, h = 0.001, zlo = -30, zhi = 30, n = 400) {
  const integrand = (z) =&gt; {
    const x = 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-z));
    const jac = x * (1 - x); // dx/dz
    const fC = densityOfTheta(x, theta) * jac;
    if (fC &lt;= 1e-300) return 0;
    const fP = densityOfTheta(x, theta + h) * jac;
    const fM = densityOfTheta(x, theta - h) * jac;
    const score = (Math.log(Math.max(fP, 1e-300)) - Math.log(Math.max(fM, 1e-300))) / (2 * h);
    return score * score * fC;
  };
  return simpson(integrand, zlo, zhi, n);
}

























